1)  Explain how the Navigator widget works in Flutter.
Ans ->
       In Flutter, the Navigator widget is used to move from one screen to another screen.
       You can think of it like pages in a book.

       When a new screen opens, it is added on top of the previous screen.
       When you go back, the top screen is removed.

       Flutter manages screens using something called a stack.

       Simple Example

       Suppose you are on a Login Screen and want to open the Home Screen.

       Navigator.push(
          context,
          MaterialPageRoute(
          builder: (context) => HomeScreen(),
           ),
         );

2) Describe the concept of named routes and their advantages over direct route navigation.
Ans ->
      Named routes are routes given with a unique name (string).
      Instead of directly writing the screen class every time, we use route names.

      Example of Named Routes
      Step 1: Define Routes
      MaterialApp(
       initialRoute: '/',
        routes: {
         '/': (context) => LoginScreen(),
         '/home': (context) => HomeScreen(),
         '/profile': (context) => ProfileScreen(),
         },
        );

3) Explain how data can be passed between screens using route arguments
Ans -> 
      Sometimes we need to send information from one screen to another screen in Flutter.

       For example:

      Sending username from Login Screen to Home Screen.
      Sending product details to Product Screen.
      Data can be passed using constructor also.
       This is called passing data between screens.

       Flutter allows us to pass data using route arguments.

       Example
       Sending Data
       Navigator.pushNamed(
       context,
        '/home',
         arguments: "Hetvi",
       );
      
      Explanation
         pushNamed() opens another screen.
         arguments: is used to send data.
         Here, "Hetvi" is being sent to the Home Screen.
